# Mutation cases for "a cost_s that is not a stopwatch reading says so".
#
# Run with
# `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/a_paced_cost_says_so_where_a_reader_looks.cases`.
#
# Six of the registry's `cost_s` values are paced figures rather than raw
# timings, and until 2026-09-02 the only place that was written was a
# comment. The consequence was measured that day: `--order-drift` reported
# `http-endpoint-ssot declared 4s ran 0s` and advised "update cost_s", while
# three lines above the number a comment already explained why the two
# disagree. Following the report would have undone a decision somebody had
# made deliberately, and it would have said the same thing about all six.
#
# PACE_NORMALISED moved that fact where the code can read it. These cases
# hold the three places it has to reach, because a map only one of them
# consults rots in the other two:
#
#   1. the prose and the data name the same gates,
#   2. the drift report changes its ADVICE for those gates,
#   3. `--mapping` publishes the answer, since that is the surface a
#      consumer is told to ask instead of scraping this file's source.
#
# Each case is a single edit, and each was observed red before landing here
# — the sweep that produced them ran twelve mutations, of which these three
# are the ones that map to a distinct consumer. The other nine are covered
# by these three plus the self-test's own cases: they weaken a message or a
# scanner detail rather than cutting a reader off.
#
# ⚠ The map emptying is NOT a defect and must not become a case. Every entry
# leaves when its gate is honestly re-measured on a quiet machine, so an
# empty PACE_NORMALISED is what finishing this work looks like. The
# self-test drives the report from a fixture for exactly that reason, and a
# case built on the live map would punish the last honest measurement for
# landing.
#
# ⚠⚠ That warning was written here and then broken here, by the case directly
# below it. It used to rename a live entry, `"w3c-go": "52s,`; on 2026-09-02
# `3f66301c79` retired w3c-go on a warm timing — an honest re-measurement,
# precisely the event the paragraph above says must not break a case. The
# anchor then matched nothing, which `scripts/mutate` calls INCONCLUSIVE and
# `scripts/gate mutation-cases` turns into a red.
#
# The re-aim is not a swap to another live key, which would rot on the next
# honest measurement: the case now INJECTS a slug that is neither a gate nor
# a comment. That fires the same equality from the other side, names no gate
# that could be retired, and survives the map emptying completely — with
# PACE_NORMALISED empty it still has something to check, which is the
# property the old case lacked.

mutation_tests -p sce-build --test gate_registry_contract
mutation_targets sce-build/tests/gate_registry_contract.rs
# Read at run time: `the_gate_registry_passes_its_own_cases` executes the
# registry as a subprocess, so an edit to it is invisible to a rebuild check.
mutation_runtime_targets tools/git-hooks/gate_registry.py

mutation_case "PACE_NORMALISED and the comments stop naming the same slugs" <<'PY'
edit("tools/git-hooks/gate_registry.py",
     'PACE_NORMALISED: dict[str, str] = {\n',
     'PACE_NORMALISED: dict[str, str] = {\n'
     '    "no-such-gate": "an entry no gate and no comment in this file carries",\n')
PY

mutation_case "the drift report gives paced costs the ordinary advice" <<'PY'
edit("tools/git-hooks/gate_registry.py",
     'if any(s in paced for s, _, _ in drift["moved"]):',
     'if False:')
PY

mutation_case "--mapping stops publishing whether a cost is paced" <<'PY'
edit("tools/git-hooks/gate_registry.py",
     'slug: dict(spec, pace_normalised=PACE_NORMALISED.get(slug))',
     'slug: spec')
PY
